{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>On Sukkot it is written: bullocks thirteen, rams two, lambs fourteen.</b> Since on Sukkot there is added joy, as Rashi explained above, for there is an additional offering and the bullocks diminish progressively so that they come to a total of seventy, corresponding to the seventy nations. And the lambs remain in their place every day, fourteen, so that they total ninety-eight, in order to nullify the ninety-eight reproofs in the Mishneh Torah. And the Rav says in Bekhor Shor that an explanation is needed why they stand every day as two rams — because on all the days of the Festival the additional offering changes each day, unlike the additional offering of Pesach. Therefore we complete the Hallel on every day of the Festival. And on the days of Pesach we do not complete it except the first two days only. And in the second chapter of Arakhin it asks: Shabbat, which is distinguished in its offerings — let us say [Hallel]? And it answers: Shabbat is not called a mo'ed. And on the day of Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim, which are days of judgment, we do not say it. And on Hanukkah we complete it because of the miracle. And on Purim, one cannot say הללויה הללו עבדי etc., for we are still servants of Ahasuerus."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:10:22.931212175Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}